I asked the minister to give the committee a general description of the activities of the national film board. I have
Supply-War Services

to confess I know nothing aibout it. I should like to know something about it because I am interested in it.
Mr. LaFLECHE: The break-down of the vote is on broad lines and answers the hon. member's question. I shall read it, and then I shall give as many details as are desired. Apart from the question of staff which is always necessary, we have items for national film programme, national film programme rural circuits, a very interesting development, I may say; the development of international circulation of films. I have already said something about that. Supplies, materials, printing and stationery; acquisition of equipment and travelling expenses. I believe that the hon. member wishes information about the work performed by the national film board. I shall read from the National Film Board Act of May 2, 1939. The duties of the commissioner, which in a sense cover the activities of the entire staff, are as follows:
The commissioner in carrying out the duties imposed upon him by this act shall at all times be under and subject to the directions of the board and shall
(a)	advise upon the making and distribution of national films designed to help Canadians in ail parts of Canada to understand -the ways of living and the problems of Canadians in other parts;
(b)	coordinate national and departmental film activities an consultation with the board and the several departments and branches of government work;
(c)	advise as to methods of securing quality, economy, efficiency and effective cooperation in the production, distribution and exhibition of government films:
(d)	advise upon and approve production, distribution and exhibition contacts and agreements in connection with film activities of the several departments of the government and, in respect of .these, act as intermediary between such departments and the bureau and between such departments and commercial firms;
(e)	advise upon all departmental expenditures in the production, distribution and exhibition of films;
(f)	represent the board in its relations with commercial newsreel and non-commercial film organizations;
(g)	advise .as to the distribution of government films in other countries;
('h) coordinate and develop information services in connection with government film activities.
Those are the authorities.
Now, if I may, I should like to answer to an even greater extent the question put by the hon. member. I have here a list headed, "National film productions completed during the fiscal year 1943-44." There is no better
[Mr. Jaqiies.l
way to answer the question put by the hon. gentleman than by giving him the names of the films completed under the authority I have just read. Again this is a very long list, and while I have no objection to reading it I am afraid you, Mr. Chairman, and the members of the committee would think I was taking too much time. Therefore with the permission of the committee I shall be very happy to put this list on record.
